---
// src/components/SectionHeading.astro — the <h2> of a case study, rendered
// with a rule under it. Wired up as the MDX `h2` override in
// pages/projects/[id].astro, so writing `## Research` in the MDX is still the
// only thing the author does.
//
// WHY THE SPAN. The rule is a border on the h2's box, and the h2's box has to
// land on a painted line. The baseline snapper moves text with a transform,
// and a transform carries the border with it — so an h2 that is both
// box-snapped and baseline-snapped would have its rule dragged back off the
// line the box-snap just put it on. Splitting the two jobs fixes it: the h2
// keeps its box (box-snap, draws the rule) and the span inside takes the
// baseline shift. Same division as .callout and its inner <p>, and .dek does
// it too — see Dek.astro.
//
// data-snap-box and data-snap are both by hand rather than from the layout's
// tagger, which cannot tell these two apart: it would put data-snap on the h2
// itself, which is the thing we are avoiding. The tagger skips
// `h2.section-heading` for that reason (see BaseLayout.astro).
//
// The numeral rides on the span, not the h2 — it belongs with the words, so
// it has to take the same baseline shift they do.
interface Props {
  id?: string;
}
const { id } = Astro.props;
---
<h2 id={id} class="section-heading" data-snap-box>
  <span class="section-heading__text" data-snap><slot /></span>
</h2>
